If you want to save the music a user creates
If you're building a music app (like a sequencer), you'll need to save the project data as JSON.
Example:
const project = {
  bpm: 120,
  notes: [
    { time: "0:0", note: "C4", duration: "8n" },
    { time: "0:2", note: "E4", duration: "8n" },
    { time: "1:0", note: "G4", duration: "4n" }
  ]
};

localStorage.setItem("myProject", JSON.stringify(project));
Later:
const saved = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("myProject"));

console.log(saved.bpm);
console.log(saved.notes);

If you want to export the audio
Tone.js doesn't automatically export audio. You can:
Record the output with the browser's MediaRecorder API.
Use Tone.Recorder to capture the audio and download it as a WAV file.
Example:
const recorder = new Tone.Recorder();
Tone.getDestination().connect(recorder);

await recorder.start();

// play your music

const recording = await recorder.stop();
The recording can then be turned into a downloadable audio file.
To save a Tone.js project, you must save its configuration or record its audio output. Since Tone.js runs entirely in the browser, data does not save automatically.
Save the Project State (Code & Settings)
To recreate the music later, save the parameters, timeline data, or source code.
Local Storage: Best for quick browser-based saving.
JSON Export: Best for downloading project files.
Database: Best for user accounts and cloud syncing.
javascript
// Example: Serializing a simple step sequencer state
const projectData = {
  bpm: Tone.Transport.bpm.value,
  notes: [["C4", "E4"], [], ["G4"], []],
  synthSettings: synth.get()
};

// Save to Local Storage
localStorage.setItem("myToneProject", JSON.stringify(projectData));

// Load from Local Storage
const savedData = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("myToneProject"));

Use code with caution.
Save the Audio Output (Recording)
To export the project as a playable audio file (like WAV), record the master output.
Tone.Recorder: Built-in utility for capturing audio.
Blob URL: Creates a temporary link to download the file.
Format: Automatically records into a high-quality buffer. [1]
javascript
// 1. Create a recorder and connect it
const recorder = new Tone.Recorder();
Tone.Destination.connect(recorder);

// 2. Start recording and playing
recorder.start();
Tone.Transport.start();

// 3. Stop recording after 4 seconds and download
setTimeout(async () => {
  Tone.Transport.stop();
  const recording = await recorder.stop();
  
  // Create download link
  const url = URL.createObjectURL(recording);
  const anchor = document.createElement("a");
  anchor.download = "my-track.webm"; // Or .wav depending on browser
  anchor.href = url;
  anchor.click();
}, 4000);

Use code with caution.
Essential Audio Elements to Track
Ensure your saving mechanism captures these core components:
Transport State: BPM, time signature, and loop points.
Instrument Settings: Oscillator types, filter frequencies, and envelope values.
Effect Chains: Delay times, reverb room sizes, and wet/dry mixes.
Sequence Data: Multi-dimensional arrays representing notes and time slots. [1, 2]
Extracting Data from Active Objects
.get() method: Call synth.get() on an active synth or effect to return a current JavaScript object of all its nested parameters (like oscillator and envelope settings). [1, 2]
JSON.stringify(): Pass the result of .get() into JSON.stringify(synth.get()) to turn those live variables into a save-ready JSON string. [1]
Manual properties: Read specific properties directly from your node instance (e.g., synth.volume.value or filter.frequency.value) and assign them to your own custom object keys.
